::ENG2_U03_L05_GP_Q01::[MLA.ENG2.R.08] Shared Information: A town can fund either bus shelters or trail repairs. The bus route serves 900 riders each week. The trail has damaged sections used by walkers and cyclists. Argument A says the town should improve bus shelters first. Question: Which evidence best supports Argument A?{
~A. The trail has damaged sections.#Incorrect. This supports the opposing argument for trail repairs.
=B. The bus route serves 900 riders each week.#Correct. This evidence directly supports the claim that bus shelters affect many residents.
~C. Walkers use the trail.#Incorrect. This supports trail repair, not bus shelters.
~D. The town can fund only one project.#Incorrect. This explains the issue but does not specifically support Argument A.
}

::ENG2_U03_L05_GP_Q02::[MLA.ENG2.R.08] Shared Information: A school survey showed 62 percent wanted more study space and 38 percent wanted more public speaking activities. Argument A supports a quiet study room. Argument B supports a debate club. Question: Which argument uses the survey data more directly?{
~A. Argument B, because debate is useful.#Incorrect. Usefulness is reasoning, but the survey majority supports study space more directly.
~B. Neither argument can use the survey.#Incorrect. Both can mention it, but one uses it more directly.
~C. Argument B, because 38 percent is larger than 62 percent.#Incorrect. That comparison is mathematically incorrect.
=D. Argument A, because 62 percent wanted more study space.#Correct. Argument A directly connects its claim to the larger survey group.
}

::ENG2_U03_L05_GP_Q03::[MLA.ENG2.R.08] Argument: "The school should add bike racks because my friend likes bikes." Question: What is the weakness in this argument?{
=A. The evidence is personal and too narrow.#Correct. One friend's preference does not strongly support a schoolwide decision.
~B. The claim is too formal.#Incorrect. The problem is not formality.
~C. The argument has too much data.#Incorrect. It has too little relevant data.
~D. The argument compares two texts.#Incorrect. It does not compare texts.
}

::ENG2_U03_L05_GP_Q04::[MLA.ENG2.R.08] Shared Information: The gym roof leaks and the auditorium microphones fail. Argument A says fix the roof first because leaks can damage the floor. Argument B says replace microphones first because assemblies are hard to hear. Question: Which evaluation is most balanced?{
~A. Argument A is correct because roofs are always more interesting.#Incorrect. This uses preference, not evidence.
~B. Argument B is wrong because microphones never matter.#Incorrect. The passage says failed microphones affect assemblies.
=C. Argument A may be more urgent because leaks can create safety and damage concerns, while Argument B addresses communication problems.#Correct. This evaluates both arguments using evidence.
~D. Both arguments are equally unsupported.#Incorrect. Both include evidence.
}

::ENG2_U03_L05_GP_Q05::[MLA.ENG2.R.08] Argument: "The town should repair the trail first because damaged sections create a safety risk for walkers and cyclists." Question: What role does the phrase "damaged sections create a safety risk" play?{
~A. It is an unrelated opinion.#Incorrect. It directly supports the claim.
=B. It is evidence and reasoning that supports the claim.#Correct. It identifies a problem and explains why repair matters.
~C. It is the opposing claim.#Incorrect. It supports the claim to repair the trail.
~D. It is a conclusion about bus shelters.#Incorrect. Bus shelters are not the focus of this argument.
}
